SaaS vs PaaS vs IaaS

Let’s simplify the three core cloud computing models—SaaS, PaaS, and IaaS—with a real-life analogy: owning a car.


🔹 IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service)
You buy a car.
You’re responsible for everything: fuel, maintenance, insurance, and driving.
You're in full control, but you also handle all the complexity.
💡 Example in Cloud: Renting servers, networking, and storage (e.g., AWS EC2).


🔹 PaaS (Platform as a Service)
You lease a car.
The provider takes care of maintenance and servicing. You just focus on driving.
Less responsibility, more focus on your core task.
💡 Example in Cloud: Using platforms to build/deploy apps (e.g., Google App Engine).


🔹 SaaS (Software as a Service)
You take a taxi.
You don’t worry about the car, fuel, or route. Just hop in and enjoy the ride.
Completely managed and effortless.
💡 Example in Cloud: Accessing software like Gmail or Zoom.


🧠 Cloud services simplify IT needs by shifting responsibilities to the provider, letting you choose how much control you want to retain.
